 "stimulus_html": "In the following exercises, different savings strategies will be compared.",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 518734,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Sam is 23 years old and has just landed her first post-college job. She creates a budget, and using the 50-30-20 budget philosophy, she sees she should save or pay down debt with $650. She decides to apply $300 per month to long-term savings. She finds an account bearing 3.75% interest compounded monthly. Sam begins investing $300 per month in that account on her 24th birthday. How much will be in the account at age 65 (41 years)?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 518735,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Sam decides instead to delay investing in the account until her 35th birthday. How much will be in the account at age 65 (30 years)?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 518736,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Sam decides to deposit the $300 per month until she turns 35 years old (11 years). She will then stop investing the $300 monthly, and just allow the money to earn interest until her 65th birthday (30 more years). How much will be in her account on her 65th birthday? Hint: First, compute the FV of the deposits. Then use that FV as the principal for a single deposit into an account bearing 3.75% interest compounded monthly.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 518737,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Compare the results of the three investment strategies. ",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  }
 ],
